---
import ConverterPage from '../components/ConverterPage.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Why are there two copy buttons?',
    a: 'Because what you see is not one copyable string. The output box shows Hindi only because the DevLys font is drawing ASCII keys. <strong>Copy DevLys code</strong> gives you the raw keys to paste into Word with the DevLys font applied; <strong>Copy as Hindi (Unicode)</strong> gives you the Unicode, for WhatsApp, email or anything on the web. Picking the wrong one is the single most common mistake on this kind of page, so both are generated and labelled rather than left to chance.',
  },
  {
    q: 'The output looks like English letters. Is that broken?',
    a: 'No, that is exactly right. DevLys text <em>is</em> ASCII - the font is what turns <code>Hkkjr</code> into <strong lang="hi">भारत</strong>. Use the Preview / Raw toggle above the output box to see it either way. The preview is purely visual and never changes what gets copied.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need the DevLys font installed?',
    a: 'To <em>use</em> the output in Word, yes - install DevLys 010 and apply it to the pasted text, or use Download .docx, which writes the font name into the file for you. To see the preview on this page you need nothing; the font is served by the site.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I type Hindi straight from my phone keypad?',
    a: 'Yes. Hindi typed on an Android or iPhone keypad, or with the Windows Hindi keyboard, is already Unicode. Paste it into the left panel and DevLys comes out on the right.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is DevLys the same as Kruti Dev?',
    a: 'Different fonts, same keyboard layout. Every key this converter produces draws the same Hindi letter in both, which was confirmed by comparing the two font files glyph by glyph. If you need Kruti Dev specifically, use <a href="/unicode-to-kruti-dev">Unicode to Kruti Dev</a> - the output is the same keystrokes, and the page names the font your recipient expects.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does my text get uploaded anywhere?',
    a: 'No. Everything runs in your browser. Nothing is sent to a server, and the page keeps working after you disconnect from the internet.',
  },
];
---

<ConverterPage
  path="/unicode-to-devlys"
  lede="Type or paste Unicode Hindi on the left and get DevLys 010 font text on the right, ready to paste into Word. Nothing is uploaded."
  faq={faq}
>
  <h2>What this converter does</h2>
  <p>
    This is the direction you need when a document has to go <em>back</em> into
    a legacy workflow: a press file, a school register, a form that was laid out
    in DevLys years ago and still has to match. You type Hindi the modern way -
    phone keypad, Windows Hindi keyboard, or pasted from anywhere on the web -
    and get DevLys keystrokes out.
  </p>
  <p>
    The output will look like nonsense Latin text, and that is correct. DevLys
    stores Hindi as ASCII and relies on the font to draw the letters. Paste it
    into Word, select it, apply DevLys 010, and it reads as Hindi.
  </p>

  <h2>The reordering, in reverse</h2>
  <p>
    Going this way the engine has to undo everything the forward direction does.
    Unicode keeps Hindi in <em>logical</em> order; DevLys keeps it in
    <em>visual</em> order. So:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The short-i matra moves back <strong>in front of</strong> its consonant -
      <strong lang="hi">कि</strong> becomes <code>fd</code>, not <code>df</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The reph moves back <strong>after</strong> the cluster it crowns, so
      <strong lang="hi">कर्म</strong> becomes <code>deZ</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Composed vowel signs are split again: <strong lang="hi">को</strong> becomes
      an aa-matra plus an e-matra, because that is how the font draws it.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Get any of those wrong and the text still <em>looks</em> like Hindi in the
    DevLys font while being subtly misspelt. Both directions are covered by the
    same test suite, and text converted to DevLys and back arrives unchanged.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this page is live now</h2>
  <p>
    DevLys 010 and Kruti Dev 010 share a keyboard layout, but "share" was checked
    rather than assumed. Every character code this converter can emit was read
    out of both font files and compared - advance width, contour count and
    bounding box - and all of them match. The two fonts do diverge in one block
    of character codes, and the conversion table deliberately never uses it.
  </p>

  <h2>How to use it</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Type or paste your Hindi into the left panel.</li>
    <li>
      Press <strong>Copy DevLys code</strong> for Word, or
      <strong>Copy as Hindi</strong> if you actually wanted the Unicode.
    </li>
    <li>
      In Word, paste and then apply the <strong>DevLys 010</strong> font - or use
      <strong>Download .docx</strong>, which sets the font for you.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    For the other direction see <a href="/devlys-to-unicode"
      >DevLys to Unicode</a
    >. If your source is Mangal, <a href="/mangal-to-devlys">Mangal to DevLys</a>
    is the same conversion under the name most government forms use.
  </p>
</ConverterPage>
